{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"maintainer-handover-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Maintainer handover track","description":"Hands an open-source project from a departing maintainer to successors in gated steps, from an access inventory and successor choice to a staged transfer, announcement and stepping back.","category":"open-source","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","operate","maintain"],"role":["maintainer","tech-lead"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text"],"output":["checklist","plan","message"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","tags":["succession","maintainer-transfer","access-control","project-handover","community-health"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["promote-contributor-to-maintainer","retire-open-source-project","write-project-governance"],"personas":["open-source-maintainer"]},"args":[{"name":"project","description":"The project, where it lives and is published, how widely it is used, and the people involved - who is leaving, who might take over, and who else has access.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"timeline","description":"When the departing maintainer wants to be fully out, and how much time they can give until then. Optional.","type":"string"}],"steps":[{"id":"inventory","file":"steps/01-inventory.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"successors","file":"steps/02-successors.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"transfer","file":"steps/03-transfer.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve"},{"id":"announce","file":"steps/04-announce.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve"},{"id":"step-back","file":"steps/05-step-back.md","stage":"maintain","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Hands the project over the way a careful maintainer would: first find every asset and credential the project depends on, then choose and vet successors, transfer access in stages with an overlap period, tell users and contributors, and finally step back cleanly. A rushed handover is how package names end up with the wrong people or a project loses its domain.\n\n<project>\n{{project}}\n</project>\n{{#timeline}}Timeline: {{timeline}}{{/timeline}}\n\nEach step produces one artifact and stops for the departing maintainer's approval or edits; later steps build on the approved versions. Never ask for, print or store passwords, tokens, recovery codes or private keys: refer to credentials by system and owner only. Never invent who holds which access; mark unknowns as `[CONFIRM: …]`. If no suitable successor exists, say so at step 2 and suggest retiring the project instead of handing it to someone unvetted. If the maintainer asks to skip approvals, confirm once, then run the remaining steps in one reply and state the choice made at each skipped gate.","steps":[{"id":"inventory","file":"steps/01-inventory.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Inventory assets and access\n\nList everything a maintainer controls, so nothing is forgotten in the handover.\n\n1. Build a table of assets by group: code hosting (organisation, repositories, admin rights, branch protection), package registries and container registries (publish rights, two-factor status, tokens in CI), CI and secrets, release signing keys, domain and DNS, docs hosting, email aliases and the security contact, chat servers and social accounts, funding platforms and any money held, trademarks or name rights, and third-party services (analytics, error tracking, CDN).\n2. For each asset record: who has access now, whether it is held only by the departing maintainer, how it is recovered if lost, and renewal dates.\n3. Flag single points of failure and anything that expires soon, such as a domain renewal.\n4. Mark every unknown as `[CONFIRM: …]` and list what the maintainer needs to check.\n\nStop and wait for corrections to the inventory. Do not discuss successors yet."},{"id":"successors","file":"steps/02-successors.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Choose and vet successors\n\nDecide who takes over, and whether anyone should.\n\n1. Ask for the candidates' records if not given: how long they have contributed, review and triage work, conduct, time available, and employer.\n2. Assess each against what the role needs, with evidence and gaps. Recommend at least two people where possible, so the project does not move from one single point of failure to another, and flag if all candidates share an employer.\n3. Watch for warning signs common in takeovers of popular packages: a newcomer with little history asking for publish rights quickly, pressure to hurry, or interest only in the package name.\n4. If no suitable successor exists, say so plainly and recommend either a call for maintainers with a deadline or retiring the project.\n5. Draft the private message inviting the chosen successors, stating what the role involves and the planned overlap.\n\nStop and wait for the maintainer to confirm successors and that they accepted."},{"id":"transfer","file":"steps/03-transfer.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: Transfer access in stages\n\nMove access from the approved inventory to the approved successors safely.\n\n1. Order the transfer: first add successors alongside the departing maintainer (repository and triage, then review and merge, then release), then registries and signing, then domain, funds and accounts last.\n2. For each asset give the action, who does it, the check that it worked (for example a successor cuts a release while the departing maintainer watches), and the date.\n3. Require two-factor authentication for every person with publish or admin rights.\n4. Plan an overlap period with at least one release done by the successors.\n5. Rotate shared secrets and tokens the departing maintainer knew, once successors control them.\n6. Update the governance or MAINTAINERS file and the security contact.\n\nStop and wait for confirmation that the transfer steps are done or scheduled. Mark anything still pending."},{"id":"announce","file":"steps/04-announce.md","stage":"operate","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 4: Announce the handover\n\nTell users and contributors what changed and why they can trust it.\n\n1. Write the announcement for the project's usual channels: who is stepping back, who the new maintainers are with their history in the project, what stays the same, what may change, and where to raise concerns.\n2. Write the README and docs updates, the funding page update, and a message to sponsors if there are any.\n3. Write a short note for downstream packagers and large known users if relevant.\n4. Make sure the announcement is published from an account users already trust (the project's own channels or the departing maintainer's), so it cannot be mistaken for a hostile takeover.\n\nStop and wait for approval of the texts."},{"id":"step-back","file":"steps/05-step-back.md","stage":"maintain","gate":"none","text":"# Step 5: Step back\n\nClose out the departing maintainer's role cleanly.\n\n1. List the access to remove from the departing maintainer at the end of the overlap, and anything they keep (for example emeritus status without admin rights).\n2. Confirm that no asset in the inventory still depends on them alone.\n3. Suggest a check-in date with the successors, one or three months later, and what to review then: releases done, security reports handled, open questions.\n4. Write a short personal thank-you note from the departing maintainer to the successors and the community, if they want one.\n\nThis is the last step."}]}
